00:08:12.590 --> 00:08:17.590
at scale because the gap between marketing claims and engineering reality is worth understanding.

00:08:18.030 --> 00:08:22.650
Everyone talks about router networks as if they magically pick the best expert every time once you

00:08:22.810 --> 00:08:28.390
have enough total parameters. In practice, the router is a tiny neural network trained jointly

00:08:28.390 --> 00:08:33.550
with the rest of the model. It uses the main loss plus an auxiliary load balancing loss that

00:08:33.550 --> 00:08:38.870
prevents the system from collapsing to just a few dominant experts. Without that auxiliary term,

00:08:39.110 --> 00:08:44.490
routers tend to converge on a handful of favorites, leaving most capacity idle. The auxiliary loss

00:08:44.490 --> 00:08:49.850
penalizes uneven token to expert assignment across each batch, usually measured by the coefficient

00:08:49.850 --> 00:08:54.930
of variation of expert load. Modern setups add noise or stochastic routing during training,

00:08:55.170 --> 00:09:01.090
then switch to deterministic top-case selection at inference time. At the scale of a 1.6 trillion

00:09:01.090 --> 00:09:07.090
parameter model with only 49 billion activated, the real costs come from expert parallelism all to

00:09:07.090 --> 00:09:12.710
all communication patterns and careful placement of experts across accelerators. The heavily compressed

00:09:12.710 --> 00:09:17.570
attention techniques mentioned in recent releases are often a direct response to the memory wall

00:09:17.570 --> 00:09:22.910
created by storing full key value states for million token contexts across dozens of experts.

00:09:23.710 --> 00:09:28.770
These systems trade exact attention for approximate retrieval plus heavy quantization just to keep

00:09:28.770 --> 00:09:33.630
activation memory manageable. The trade-offs are real, stronger load balancing can slightly hurt

00:09:33.630 --> 00:09:38.950
final loss because it forces the model to use suboptimal experts on some tokens. Aggressive

00:09:38.950 --> 00:09:44.490
compression of attention states can degrade needle and haystack retrieval once context grows beyond

00:09:44.490 --> 00:09:49.590
a few hundred thousand tokens. The quality advantage of these architectures tends to be largest

00:09:49.590 --> 00:09:55.770
in the 30 to 200 billion active parameter regime. Beyond that, the returns diminish and communication

00:09:55.770 --> 00:10:00.930
overhead can start to dominate, so when should you actually reach for one of these versus a dense

00:10:00.930 --> 00:10:06.390
model of similar activated size? Prefer the sparse route if your workload has high variance in token

00:10:06.390 --> 00:10:11.970
difficulties such as coding math or multilingual text, and you have infrastructure that can handle

00:10:11.970 --> 00:10:17.470
the all-too-all traffic. The gotcha that bites most teams is treating the router as an afterthought

00:10:17.470 --> 00:10:22.650
during fine-tuning. Without retuning the auxiliary loss and possibly reintroducing noise,

00:10:22.890 --> 00:10:27.710
the specialization learned in pre-training collapses, and you lose the speed and quality advantage

00:10:27.710 --> 00:10:35.490
you paid for. If you have not tried the Quinn 3.6 to 35 BA 3B in Q4 or Q5 quantization inside

